{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015hHkpCZcRLXvQ7neQRoiJp","type":"tool_result","content":"# The first-moment lower bound on the pathwidth of random graphs: the asymptotic half, on paper\n\n*2026-10-31. ANALYST, with the CHECKER attacking every inequality (`reports/scratch/first_moment/gossip.md`).\nScripts: `reports/scratch/first_moment/beta_star.py`, `finite_bound.py`; logs beside them.*\n\nThis document takes the finite, deterministic inequality proved in Lean\n(`lean/MOSPFormalization/FirstMoment.lean`, `weightedSum_graphsOfPathwidthLe_le`) and\ndoes the analysis the survey (`reports/random_graph_pathwidth_survey.md` §1.4, §3) left\non paper: the exponential decay below `β*(c)·n` in the sparse case, the threshold\n`c > 4 ln 2`, the asymptotics of `β*(c)`, the `n − (4 + ε) log_b n` bound in the dense\ncase, the numerical values, and the finite inequality against the pilot data.\nEverything starts from one inequality and uses nothing probabilistic beyond it.\n\n**The starting point** (Lean, `weightedSum_graphsOfPathwidthLe_le`; `k < n`, `0 ≤ p ≤ 1`):\n\n```\nP(pw(G(n, p)) ≤ k) ≤ C(n, t) · C(n − t, t − 1) · (1 − p)^(t(t − 1)),     t = (n − k + 1) div 2 = ⌈(n − k)/2⌉.   (0)\n```\n\nThe factor `C(n, t)·C(n − t, t − 1)` is the trinomial coefficient\n`n! / (t! (t − 1)! (n − 2t + 1)!)`: the number of ordered pairs `(A, R)` of disjoint\nvertex sets with `|A| = t`, `|R| = t − 1`, which is what the Lean proof counts\n(`card_emptyPairIndices`) before the union bound. **The entropy term below comes from\nbounding this trinomial by one application of the multinomial entropy bound, not from\ntwo binomial bounds** (the two routes give the same number, see Remark 2.1).\n\nNotation: `ln` is the natural logarithm; `φ(x) = −x ln x` with `φ(0) = 0`;\n`H(x, y, z) = φ(x) + φ(y) + φ(z)` for `x + y + z = 1` (the trinomial entropy in nats);\n`a = (1 − β)/2`; and\n\n```\nδ(β, c) = c a² − H(a, a, 1 − 2a) = c a² + 2a ln a + (1 − 2a) ln(1 − 2a).\n```\n\n---\n\n## 1. Statements\n\n**Theorem A (sparse case, finite form).** Let `c > 0` and `0 < β < 1`, `a = (1 − β)/2`.\nFor every integer `n ≥ max(3, c)`,\n\n```\nP( pw(G(n, c/n)) ≤ βn ) ≤ exp( −δ(β, c)·n + 3 ln n + c·a ) ≤ e^(c/2) · n³ · e^(−δ(β, c)·n).      (A)\n```\n\nIn particular, if `δ(β, c) > 0` the probability is exponentially small in `n`.\n\n**Theorem A′ (the admissible region).** For `c ≤ 4 ln 2` there is no `β ∈ (0, 1)` with\n`δ(β, c) > 0`. For `c > 4 ln 2` the function `a ↦ H(a, a, 1 − 2a) − c a²` has exactly one\nzero `a*(c)` in `(0, 1/2)`, and `δ(β, c) > 0` holds exactly for `0 < β < β*(c) := 1 − 2a*(c)`.\nHence, writing `β*(c) = 0` for `c ≤ 4 ln 2`: **`pw(G(n, c/n)) ≥ βn` with probability\n`1 − O(n³ e^(−δn))` for every `β < β*(c)`, and `β*(c) > 0` if and only if `c > 4 ln 2 ≈ 2.7726`.**\n`β*(c)` is the root in `(0, 1)` of\n\n```\n−(1 − β) ln((1 − β)/2) − β ln β = c (1 − β)² / 4.\n```\n\n*The threshold `4 ln 2` is a property of the method, not of pathwidth* (CHECKER, gossip\n05:20): it is the first-moment threshold for an edge-free balanced bipartition\n----\n39:**Theorem A (sparse case, finite form).** Let `c > 0` and `0 < β < 1`, `a = (1 − β)/2`.\n48:**Theorem A′ (the admissible region).** For `c ≤ 4 ln 2` there is no `β ∈ (0, 1)` with\n66:**Theorem A″ (asymptotics of the threshold).** For every `c ≥ 14`,\n77:**Theorem B (dense case).** Fix `0 < p < 1`, `b = 1/(1 − p)`, `ε > 0`. For every `n ≥ b^(4/ε)`\n95:**Lemma 1 (multinomial entropy bound).** For nonnegative integers `i + j + l = n`,\n102:*Elements of Information Theory*, 2nd ed., Theorem 11.1.3, for a type on three symbols;\n112:**Remark 2.1 (which form).** I apply Lemma 1 once, to the trinomial with\n121:**Lemma 2.** For `0 ≤ p ≤ 1` and every integer `m ≥ 0`, `(1 − p)^m ≤ exp(−p m)`.\n126:**Lemma 3 (shift lemma for `φ`).** For `0 ≤ x, y ≤ 1` with `d = |x − y| ≤ 1/e`,\n147:**Lemma 4 (the rounding of `t`).** Let `0 < β < 1`, `a = (1 − β)/2`, `k = ⌊βn⌋` and\n155:### 2.2 Proof of Theorem A\n158:`{pw ≤ k}`, and `k < n` so (0) applies. Write `θ = t − an ∈ [0, 1)` (Lemma 4) and\n165:respectively (`|θ| < 1`, `|θ − 1| ≤ 1`, `|1 − 2θ| ≤ 1`). Since `n ≥ 3 > e`, Lemma 3 applies to\n173:By Lemma 1 on the trinomial, `C(n, t)·C(n − t, t − 1) ≤ exp(n H(u, v, w))`, hence by (1)\n179:By Lemma 2 with `p = c/n`, `m = t(t − 1)`, then Lemma 4,\n194:applications of Lemma 3 with shifts `≤ 1/n` (one per coordinate of the type); `c·a` is\n195:the `−an` in `t(t − 1) ≥ a²n² − an` (Lemma 4). Lemma 1 and Lemma 2 contribute no error at\n198:### 2.3 Proof of Theorem A′\n231:### 2.4 Proof of Theorem A″\n278:### 2.5 Proof of Theorem B\n300:**Theorem B is Turner's Theorem 2.1 for bandwidth, re-proved for pathwidth with the same count** (the direction matters: `pw ≤ bw`, so a bandwidth lower bound says nothing about pathwidth; what carries over is the pair count, and the pathwidth empty-pair lemma does the rest). Turner (1986),\n302:Lemma 2.1 is the empty-pair lemma for bandwidth (`bw ≤ n − 2k` gives an empty pair of sizes\n303:`k, k`, the first and last `k` positions), Lemma 2.2 the first moment\n304:`P(bw ≤ n − 2k) ≤ [(en/k)(1 − p)^(k/2)]^(2k)`, and Theorem 2.1 states `bw > n − 4 log_b n`\n305:almost surely for fixed `p`. Since `pw ≤ bw`, our Theorem B *implies* his Theorem 2.1 and\n309:(The entropy bound of Lemma 1 would sharpen the constant in front of `ln² n` but not the\n323:Theorem A′. Comparison with the survey's table (§7(a), three decimals, computed there by a\n324:`10⁻⁴` scan) and with the two asymptotic formulas of Theorem A″:\n348:Theorem A″ was verified for every integer `c` from `14` to `199` and at `c = 500, 10³, 10⁴, 10⁵, 10⁶`\n366:*Turner (1986)*, *SIAM J. Comput.* 15(2):561–580, §2, for **bandwidth**: Lemma 2.1 (`bw ≤ n − 2k`\n367:forces an empty pair of sizes `k, k`), Lemma 2.2 (`P(bw ≤ n − 2k) ≤ [(en/k)(1 − p)^(k/2)]^(2k)`,\n368:by `C(n, k) ≤ (en/k)^k`), Theorem 2.1 (`bw > n − 4 log_b n` a.s. for fixed `p` — our Theorem B's\n369:constant, for the larger parameter) and Theorem 2.2 (`bw ≥ (1 − ε) n` for `p = c ln n / n`). He\n370:states nothing at `p = c/n`, and then leaves `G(n, p)` for a planted model. His Lemma 2.2 at\n380:Math.* 235:245–253, Lemma 2 (pp. 249–250) and Lemma 3 (g5) (p. 252), for **vertex separation**. Lemma 2 shows\n383:and a Chernoff bound on the `A–B` edges; Lemma 3 (g5) then observes, for any layout, that\n392:full; Díaz–Petit–Serna 2002 *ACM Comput. Surv.* §8.1 Theorem 8.1, which restates DPST\n419:| `c` | **`β*(c)` (here, pw)** | DPST Lemma 3 as stated, `1/2 − √(3(1 + ln 3)/c)` | DPST with the prefix at `(1 − ε)n`, `1 − 2√(3(1 + ln 3)/c)` | DPST's `3^n` count with the exact pair probability, `1 − 2√(ln 3/c)` | Perarnau–Serra 2014 (tw), `1 − √(9 ln 3/(2c))` |\n494:`3 ln n + c a ≈ 12.3 + 3.3` nats, plus the Stirling factor of Lemma 1 (about `2` decades)\n495:and Lemma 2's `p² t²/2 ≈ c² a²/2 ≈ 5` nats — some ten decades in all, matching","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"79d5ae08-5614-4985-8469-5dab4f20e782","uuid":"90fcfc20-2652-427e-9279-6ec82721b229","timestamp":"2026-11-01T13:10:05.234Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"# The first-moment lower bound on the pathwidth of random graphs: the asymptotic half, on paper\n\n*2026-10-31. ANALYST, with the CHECKER attacking every inequality (`reports/scratch/first_moment/gossip.md`).\nScripts: `reports/scratch/first_moment/beta_star.py`, `finite_bound.py`; logs beside them.*\n\nThis document takes the finite, deterministic inequality proved in Lean\n(`lean/MOSPFormalization/FirstMoment.lean`, `weightedSum_graphsOfPathwidthLe_le`) and\ndoes the analysis the survey (`reports/random_graph_pathwidth_survey.md` §1.4, §3) left\non paper: the exponential decay below `β*(c)·n` in the sparse case, the threshold\n`c > 4 ln 2`, the asymptotics of `β*(c)`, the `n − (4 + ε) log_b n` bound in the dense\ncase, the numerical values, and the finite inequality against the pilot data.\nEverything starts from one inequality and uses nothing probabilistic beyond it.\