{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HbAnHwyQmwW8Tf3hvKzd76","type":"tool_result","content":"tests/test_first_moment.py\n----\n65:    def from_matrix(matrix: List[List[int]] | np.ndarray, name: str = \"\") -> MOSPInstance:\n79:        lines = [self.name, f\"{self.n_customers} {self.n_patterns}\"]\n80:        for row in self.matrix:\n86:        return set(np.where(self.matrix[:, pattern] == 1)[0].tolist())\n90:        return set(np.where(self.matrix[customer, :] == 1)[0].tolist())\n----\n154:- [ ] **03 The clumping decomposed: pairs, separators, closed pairs and move-classes on instances with exact pathwidth.**\n155-  - **Question.** B of the brief. On a graph of pathwidth `k`, how many\n156-    exact-size empty pairs `X₁` are there, how many distinct separators\n157-    `X₂` among them, how many closed pairs `X₃` (sides `≥` forced sizes,\n158-    `|S| ≤ k`), and how many move-classes `X₄` (connected components of the\n159-    graph on empty pairs where two pairs are adjacent if one vertex changed\n160-    class or two vertices swapped classes)? How fast do `X₁/X₂`, `X₁/X₃`,\n161-    `X₁/X₄` grow with `n`, in nats per vertex, against the `0.066` that a\n162-    `0.02` gain in `β` needs at `c = 10` (plan §0)?\n163-  - **Method.** (a) *Instances.* Fresh `G(n, c/n)` at `n ∈ {14, 16, 18, 20,\n164-    22, 24}`, `c ∈ {4, 6, 8, 10}`, 200 per cell, seeds recorded; exact\n165-    pathwidth by `learning.first_moment.vertex_separation_brute` (`n ≤ 16`)\n166-    and by the C program `pathwidth_solver/cli/pathwidth -l -t 60` on\n167-    graph6 (`networkx.to_graph6_bytes`; `n ≥ 18`), the layout kept in the\n168-    CSV; the pilot's `n = 20` cells (137 proved each, layouts in\n169-    `reports/scratch/gnp_pilot/results.csv`) as the overlap check at one\n170-    size; and certified random covers from `learning/data/ensemble/results.csv`\n171-    (`fixed`, `n ∈ {10, 15, 20, 25}` where present, `d ∈ {4, 6}`, `m = n`,\n172-    regenerated from seed; `pw = optimum − 1`). No instance of the record\n173-    is re-solved. (b) *Enumeration.* All exact-size empty pairs at `k = pw`:\n174-    enumerate `t`-sets `A` with `|N[A]| ≤ n − (N − t)` by the pruned search\n175-    of `satisfiability/expansion_bound.py::_f_exact` (adapted to list the\n176-    sets rather than the minimum), then every `(N − t)`-subset `R` of\n177-    `V ∖ N[A]`; `X₂` = distinct `V ∖ (A ∪ R)`; `X₃` by closing every pair\n178-    and deduplicating, and independently by direct enumeration of closed\n179-    pairs at `n ≤ 16`; `X₄` by union–find over the adjacency defined above.\n180-    Also at `k = pw + 1` and `pw + 2` (the counts grow; the ratios are what\n181-    matter) and `k = pw − 1` (`X₁` may be 0: report the share). (c) *Rates.*\n182-    Per cell, the geometric mean and median of `X₁`, `X₁/X₂`, `X₁/X₃`,\n183-    `X₁/X₄`; the slope of `log(geometric mean)` against `n` at fixed `c`\n184-    with its standard error; the same on the covers against `n`. Beside\n185-    them the second-moment prediction for `G(n, c/n)` at `β = pw/n` (the\n186-    `3 × 3` overlap table of `learning.first_moment.second_moment_rate`\n187-    rewritten for Bernoulli edges: a one-function addition\n188-    `second_moment_rate_gnp(a, c)`, tested against enumeration at `n = 10`).\n189-    (d) *The overlap distribution.* For 20 graphs per cell at `n = 20`,\n190-    `c = 10`: the overlap table of every pair of empty pairs, histogrammed\n191-    by `|A ∩ A'|/n`, `|A ∩ R'|/n`, `|A ∩ S'|/n` — is the extremal table of\n192-    §8.5 (a) (sides exchanged with `S`) what instances show? About 3\n193-    core-hours on 8 workers at nice 19; sample down and say so if the 4 h\n194-    cap approaches.\n----\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"Regression gate for loop0011 (the separator first moment).\n\nPasses only if all five hold:\n1. `lake build` in lean/ succeeds;\n2. every `.lean` file under lean/MOSPFormalization/ (top level, Complex/ and\n   Search/) is imported by lean/MOSPFormalization.lean, so the build really\n   checks it;\n3. the number of `sorry` in Lean *code* (comments and docstrings stripped) is\n   at most the baseline (0: the development has none since Appendix E) plus\n   the entries in allowed_sorries.txt, and no `axiom` declaration exists;\n4. `python -m pytest tests/ -q -x` passes, and so does `pytest` in\n   pathwidth_solver/;\n5. the paper builds with no undefined references (`make -C paper1/latex check`).\n\n    python3 Ralph_Loops/loop0011/gate.py\n\"\"\"\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport re\nimport subprocess\nimport sys\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nHERE = Path(__file__).resolve().parent\nREPO = HERE.parents[1]\nLEAN = REPO / \"lean\"\nBASELINE_SORRIES = 0\nALLOWED = HERE / \"allowed_sorries.txt\"\nPAPER = REPO / \"paper1\" / \"latex\"\n\n\ndef strip_comments(src: str) -> str:\n    out, i, depth = [], 0, 0\n    while i < len(src):\n        if src.startswith(\"/-\", i):\n            depth += 1; i += 2; continue\n        if depth and src.startswith(\"-/\", i):\n            depth -= 1; i += 2; continue\n        if depth:\n            i += 1; continue\n        if src.startswith(\"--\", i):\n            j = src.find(\"\\n\", i); i = len(src) if j < 0 else j; continue\n        if src[i] == '\"':\n            j = i + 1\n            while j < len(src) and src[j] != '\"':\n                j += 2 if src[j] == \"\\\\\" else 1\n            i = j + 1; continue\n        out.append(src[i]); i += 1\n    return \"\".join(out)\n\n\ndef main() -> int:\n    ok = True\n    b = subprocess.run([\"lake\", \"build\"], cwd=LEAN, capture_output=True, text=True, errors=\"replace\")\n    tail = \"\\n\".join((b.stdout + b.stderr).splitlines()[-25:])\n    if b.returncode != 0:\n        print(\"GATE FAIL: lake build\\n\" + tail); return 1\n    print(\"lake build: ok\")","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"79d5ae08-5614-4985-8469-5dab4f20e782","uuid":"51399f9d-388c-46fe-88a3-7461b5ee2437","timestamp":"2026-11-01T13:11