{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-fable-5-1","id":"msg_011CgcGhugtWivKWTzjAQVe8","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_011JhMCDxrLMDnm1ij21eGpE","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cat >> reports/separator_moment.md <<'EOF'\n\n---\n\n## 2. The deterministic lemmas: balanced separator, its relaxation, the closed pair (item 02, 2026-11-01)\n\n**Question (A of the brief).** Exactly which structures does\n`pw(G) ≤ k < n` force, in a form a first moment can count once per\nseparator? Three, each one line from `exists_empty_pair_of_layout`\n(`EmptyPair.lean`), and each checked by brute force before being called a\nlemma: on every graph on 1–9 vertices from `nauty-geng` (288,266 graphs),\non 20,000 random `G(n, p)` graphs at `n = 10, 11, 12` and on 2,000 random\nclique covers at 4–10 customers (§2.4). **Zero counterexamples.** The\nkill criterion (a counterexample to L1, L1′ or L2) is **not met**.\n\nNotation, for the whole section: `G` is a graph on the vertex set `V`,\n`|V| = n`; `N[X] = X ∪ N(X)` is the closed neighbourhood and\n`N(X) = N[X] ∖ X` the *outer* boundary of `X`; a pair `(A, R)` of disjoint\nsets is **empty** when no edge joins `A` to `R`, equivalently\n`R ∩ N[A] = ∅`; `k` is an integer with `pw(G) ≤ k < n`,\n`N = n − k`, `t = ⌈N/2⌉`, so that `t ≥ N − t ≥ 0` and `t ≥ 1`. Nothing in\nthis section is probabilistic.\n\n### 2.1 L1 — the balanced separator at exact sizes\n\n**Lemma L1.** If `pw(G) ≤ k < n`, there are pairwise disjoint `A, R, S`\nwith `A ∪ R ∪ S = V`, `|A| = t`, `|R| = N − t`, `|S| = k` and no `A–R`\nedge.\n\n*Proof.* (Layout step) By `vertexSeparation_eq_pathwidth` there is a\nlayout `σ` with `vs(σ) = pw(G) ≤ k`. (Prefix step) Apply\n`exists_empty_pair_of_layout` at position `i = t − 1`, which is `< n`\nbecause `t ≤ N ≤ n`: the prefix `A` of size `t` and the inactive suffix\n`R₀` are disjoint, no edge joins them, and\n`n ≤ |A| + |R₀| + vs(σ) ≤ t + |R₀| + k`, i.e. `|R₀| ≥ N − t`\n(`card_inactiveSuffix`). (Shrink step) Take `R ⊆ R₀` with `|R| = N − t`;\nthe edge condition is universally quantified over `R₀`, so it holds on\n`R`, and `R ⊆ R₀` is disjoint from `A`. (Complement step)\n`S = V ∖ (A ∪ R)` has `n − t − (N − t) = k` vertices and is disjoint from\nboth. ∎\n\n*Remarks.* (i) The sizes are exact: `|R| = N − t = ⌊N/2⌋`, which is the\nitem's `n − t − k`. The Lean corollary `exists_empty_pair` rounds it to\n`|R| ≥ t − 1`, which coincides with `N − t` for `N` odd and is one less\nfor `N` even; the count on record (`FirstMoment.lean`,\n`card_emptyPairIndices`) uses `|R| = t − 1`, and plan §0 correction 2 is\nabout this rounding. L1 is the sharper form and is what §2.4 checks.\n(ii) `S` is the active suffix at position `t − 1` padded to exactly `k`\nvertices with vertices of `R₀`; which vertices are moved is immaterial,\nand the lemma says nothing about `S` beyond its size — that is what L2\nadds.\n\n### 2.2 L1′ — the separator events `E_bip` and `E_relax`\n\nFor a `k`-set `S ⊆ V` write `G − S` for the subgraph induced on\n`V ∖ S` (which has `N` vertices) and define two events:\n\n    E_bip(S):   the components of G − S can be grouped into two classes\n                whose vertex counts are t and N − t;\n    E_relax(S): no component of G − S has more than t vertices.\n\n**Lemma L1′.** (a) If `pw(G) ≤ k < n`, some `k`-set `S` satisfies\n`E_bip(S)`. (b) For every `k`-set `S`, `E_bip(S)` implies `E_relax(S)`.\n(c) For every `k`-set `S`, `E_bip(S)` holds iff `G` has an exact-size\nempty pair `(A, R)` (`|A| = t`, `|R| = N − t`, no `A–R` edge) with\n`V ∖ (A ∪ R) = S`. Hence *\"some `k`-set is in `E_bip`\"* and *\"an\nexact-size empty pair exists\"* are the same event; the two counts `X₂`\n(separators) and `X₁` (pairs) count it differently. (d) The inclusion in\n(b) is strict: on the graph `3K₂` (six vertices, edges `03, 14, 25`) with\n`k = 0`, `S = ∅` satisfies `E_relax` (components of sizes `2, 2, 2 ≤ t = 3`)\nand not `E_bip` (no sub-family of `{2, 2, 2}` sums to `3`). This is the\nfirst graph on six vertices in `nauty-geng` order with a `k`-set in\n`E_relax ∖ E_bip` (`check.log`, first line); its pathwidth is `1`, so at\n`k = pw` the two events agree on it.\n\n*Proof.* (a) Take `A, R, S` from L1. (Connectivity step) A component `C`\nof `G − S` is connected and lies in `A ∪ R`; if it met both `A` and `R`,\nsome edge of `C` — a path inside `C` from a vertex of `A` to a vertex of\n`R` has a first `A–R` edge — would join `A` to `R`, contradicting\nemptiness. So `C ⊆ A` or `C ⊆ R`. (Grouping step) The components inside\n`A` partition `A` and sum to `t`; those inside `R` sum to `N − t`.\n(b) Each component lies in a class of size `t` or `N − t ≤ t`.\n(c, ⇒) Let `A` be the union of the class of size `t` and `R` the union\nof the other; they partition `V ∖ S`, have the right sizes, and an edge\nof `G − S` joins two vertices of one component, hence of one class, so\nno edge joins `A` to `R` (an `A–R` edge of `G` would have both ends\noutside `S`, so it would be an edge of `G − S`). (c, ⇐) is (a)'s\nargument applied to the given pair. (d) is checked directly. ∎\n\n*Remark.* `E_relax` is the Lean-cheap and LDP-shaped form (plan §0,\ncorrection 3; §1.6): it is a statement about component sizes only, and\n`P_N(E_relax)` on `G(N, p)` is the lower tail of the largest component.\n`E_bip` is the event the pair count actually witnesses, and items 05–06\ncompute `P_N(E_bip)` from the full component-size partition, not from\nthe largest component alone. On the graphs checked the two differ at\n`k = pw` on `0.3%` of graphs at 7 vertices and `1.9%` at 9 (§2.4,\ncolumn \"`E_relax ⊋ E_bip`\"), so at these sizes the relaxation is nearly\nfree; nothing here says what it costs at the scale of the rate.\n\n### 2.3 L2 — the closed pair, by the alternating closure\n\n**Lemma L2.** Let `(A, R)` be an empty pair with `|A| ≥ t`,\n`|R| ≥ N − t` and `|V ∖ (A ∪ R)| ≤ k`. Put\n\n    R* = V ∖ N[A],      A* = V ∖ N[R*],      S* = V ∖ (A* ∪ R*).\n\nThen: (i) `A ⊆ A*` and `R ⊆ R*` (monotone); (ii) `(A*, R*)` is an empty\npair; (iii) `R* = V ∖ N[A*]` and `A* = V ∖ N[R*]` (a **closed pair**), so\nthe alternating closure `R₁ = V ∖ N[A]`, `A₁ = V ∖ N[R₁]`, `R₂ = V ∖ N[A₁]`,\n`A₂ = V ∖ N[R₂]`, … is constant from `(A₁, R₁) = (A*, R*)` on — it stops\nafter **one round**, not after an unbounded iteration; (iv)\n`S* = N(A*) ∩ N(R*)`; (v) `|A*| ≥ t`, `|R*| ≥ N − t`, `|S*| ≤ k`.\nConversely, every closed pair `(A, R)` (one with `A = V ∖ N[R]` and\n`R = V ∖ N[A]`) is empty with `V ∖ (A ∪ R) = N(A) ∩ N(R)`, and is\ndetermined by either side.\n\n*Proof.* (Galois step) For any `X, Y ⊆ V`: `Y ⊆ V ∖ N[X]` iff `Y ∩ X = ∅`\nand no edge joins `X` to `Y`, iff `X ⊆ V ∖ N[Y]`, by the symmetry of\nadjacency; and `X ⊆ X′` implies `N[X] ⊆ N[X′]` (monotone step).\n(i) `(A, R)` is empty, so `R ⊆ V ∖ N[A] = R*` by the Galois step.\n`R* ∩ N[A] = ∅` by definition, so `(A, R*)` is empty and the Galois step\ngives `A ⊆ V ∖ N[R*] = A*`. (ii) `A* ∩ N[R*] = ∅` by definition.\n(iii) From (ii) and the Galois step, `R* ⊆ V ∖ N[A*]`; from `A ⊆ A*` and\nthe monotone step, `V ∖ N[A*] ⊆ V ∖ N[A] = R*`. So `R* = V ∖ N[A*]`, and\n`A* = V ∖ N[R*]` is the definition. Hence `R₂ = V ∖ N[A₁] = R₁` and\n`A₂ = V ∖ N[R₂] = A₁`, and by induction every later term equals these.\n(iv) `v ∈ S*` iff `v ∉ A*` and `v ∉ R*`; `v ∉ A* = V ∖ N[R*]` means\n`v ∈ N[R*]`, which with `v ∉ R*` is `v ∈ N(R*)`; symmetrically\n`v ∈ N(A*)`. Conversely a vertex of `N(A*) ∩ N(R*)` is outside `A*` and\noutside `R*` by the definition of the outer boundary. (v) From (i),\n`|A*| ≥ |A| ≥ t` and `|R*| ≥ |R| ≥ N − t`; then\n`|S*| = n − |A*| − |R*| ≤ n − t − (N − t) = k`. The converse is (ii)–(iv)\nread with `A* = A`, `R* = R`; \"determined by either side\" is\n`R = V ∖ N[A]`. ∎\n\n*Remarks.* (i) **The fixpoint is a representative, not the\nrepresentative.** Closing `R` first gives `(V ∖ N[R], V ∖ N[V ∖ N[R]])`,\nwhich can differ from `(A*, R*)`: on the graphs checked the two maps\ndisagree on `10%` of exact-size pairs at 7–9 vertices (§2.4, column\n\"closures differ\"). A union bound over closed pairs needs only *a*\nmap from exact-size pairs to closed pairs of the forced sizes, and L2\ngives one (`A` first; the mirror is another); it does not need the map\nto be canonical, and this document never says it is. (ii) The item\nexpected an iteration `A ⊆ A₁ ⊆ A₂ ⊆ …`; (iii) says the chain is\n`A ⊆ A₁ = A₂ = …`, `R ⊆ R₁ = R₂ = …`. Monotonicity holds as asserted\n(with equality from the second step), which is what the check verifies\nat every step — the item's expectation is corrected, not contradicted.\n(iii) Because every closed pair is determined by `A`, the closed pairs\nof the forced sizes (`X₃` below) are enumerated exactly by running over\nall `A ⊆ V`, setting `R = V ∖ N[A]` and testing `A = V ∖ N[R]` and the\nsizes; no pair is reached twice. (iv) The image of the exact-size pairs\nunder the `A`-first closure (`X₃′`) can be a proper subset of `X₃`: a\nclosed pair of the forced sizes contains exact-size sub-pairs, but\nclosing a sub-pair may land on a *larger* closed pair (its `V ∖ N[A′]`\ncontains `V ∖ N[A]`). Both counts are valid first moments; item 03\nmeasures both.\n\n### 2.4 The check, before any of the above was called a lemma\n\n`reports/scratch/separator_moment/02_lemmas/check.py` (log beside it),\non the functions of `learning/separator_moment.py` (tested in\n`tests/test_separator_moment.py`, which also re-enumerates every\nlabelled graph to 6 vertices and pins the subset DP to\n`learning.first_moment.vertex_separation_brute` and\n`fixed_parameter_algorithm.pathwidth.compute_pathwidth`). For each graph,\n`k = pw(G)` by the subset DP; then\n\n* **L1**: `X₁ > 0`, where `X₁` is the number of ordered exact-size empty\n  pairs (`|A| = t`, `|R| = N − t`; for `N` even `(A, R)` and `(R, A)` are\n  both counted);\n* **L1′**: `X₂ > 0`, `X₂ ≤ X₂′`, and `X₁ > 0 ⟺ X₂ > 0`, where `X₂`, `X₂′`\n  are the numbers of `k`-sets `S` in `E_bip` and `E_relax`, computed from\n  the components of `G − S` and a subset-sum over their sizes;\n* **L2**: from every exact-size pair (all of them when `X₁ ≤ 200`, the\n  first 200 otherwise), the closure sequence is monotone at every step,\n  the second round repeats the first, the fixpoint is closed, empty, of\n  the forced sizes, with `S* = N(A*) ∩ N(R*)`, and lies in `X₃` (all\n  closed pairs of the forced sizes, by the enumeration of remark (iii));\n  the share of pairs whose `A`-first and `R`-first fixpoints differ;\n* **L3** (covers only): a pair is empty iff no pattern meets both sides\n  (on every exact-size pair and on 20 random disjoint pairs per\n  instance), and every customer of `S*` shares a pattern with `A*` and\n  with `R*`;\n* at `k = pw − 1`: whether an exact-size pair (equivalently a `k`-set in\n  `E_bip`) and whether a `k`-set in `E_relax` still exist — the necessary\n  condition's slack at these sizes.\n\nTABLE_PLACEHOLDER\n\n### 2.5 L3 — the MOSP forms\n\nLet the instance have customers `C` (`|C| = n`) and patterns\n`S_1, …, S_m ⊆ C`; its MOSP graph `mospGraph` (`MOSPGraph.lean`;\n`coverGraph` in `CliqueCoverMoment.lean`) joins two distinct customers\niff some pattern contains both, and `mospValue = pw(mospGraph) + 1`\n(`MOSPGraph.lean`, both directions). With `k = mospValue − 1 < n`:\n\n**Lemma L3.** (a) For disjoint `A, R ⊆ C`, `(A, R)` is an empty pair of\n`mospGraph` iff no pattern meets both `A` and `R`\n(`isEmptyPair_coverGraph_iff`). (b) L1, L1′ and L2 hold for `mospGraph`\nwith `S` a set of `k` customers; in particular every instance with\n`mospValue ≤ k + 1` has `k` customers whose removal leaves customer\nclasses of sizes `t` and `N − t` such that no pattern meets both classes.\n(c) In a closed pair `(A*, R*)` of `mospGraph`, a customer `c` lies in\n`S* = N(A*) ∩ N(R*)` iff `c ∉ A* ∪ R*`, some pattern contains `c` and a\ncustomer of `A*`, and some pattern contains `c` and a customer of `R*`.\n\n*Proof.* (a) is the cited lemma. (b) is L1, L1′, L2 applied to the graph\n`mospGraph`, with `k ≥ pw` given by the equality theorem. (c) `c ∈ N(X)`\nfor `mospGraph` means `c ∉ X` and `c` is adjacent to some `x ∈ X`, i.e.\nsome pattern contains both `c` and `x`; apply L2 (iv). ∎\n\nThe check of §2.4 runs L1, L1′, L2 on the MOSP graphs of 2,000 random\ncovers and tests (a) and (c) directly against the matrix.\n\n### 2.6 Lean-ready statements for item 10\n\nAll over `{V : Type*} [Fintype V] [DecidableEq V] (G : SimpleGraph V)\n[DecidableRel G.Adj]`, in the vocabulary of `EmptyPair.lean`\n(`closedNbhd`, `IsEmptyPair` from `FirstMoment.lean`), with `n = Fintype.card V`,\n`t = (n − k + 1) / 2`, `N − t = (n − k) / 2` in `ℕ` arithmetic.\n\n| name | hypotheses | conclusion | rests on |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `exists_balanced_separator` (L1) | `pathwidth G ≤ k`, `k < n` | `∃ A R S : Finset V`, pairwise disjoint, `A ∪ R ∪ S = univ`, `A.card = t`, `R.card = N − t`, `S.card = k`, `∀ u ∈ A, ∀ v ∈ R, ¬ G.Adj u v` | `vertexSeparation_eq_pathwidth`, `exists_empty_pair_of_layout` at `i = t − 1`, `Finset.exists_subset_card_eq`, `Finset.card_sdiff` |\n| `IsBalancedBip G S k` (def) | — | `∃ A R, Disjoint A R ∧ A ∪ R = univ \\ S ∧ A.card = t ∧ R.card = N − t ∧ IsEmptyPair G A R` — the pair form of `E_bip`, which L1′ (c) shows equivalent to the component form | — |\n| `exists_isBalancedBip` (L1′ a) | `pathwidth G ≤ k`, `k < n` | `∃ S, S.card = k ∧ IsBalancedBip G S k` | `exists_balanced_separator` |\n| `connectedComponent_subset_side` (connectivity step) | `IsEmptyPair G A R`, `C` a connected component of `G.induce (A ∪ R)` | `C ⊆ A ∨ C ⊆ R` | induction on `SimpleGraph.Walk` in the induced graph |\n| `isBalancedBip_iff_components` (L1′ c) | `S.card = k` | `IsBalancedBip G S k ↔` the components of `G.induce (univ \\ S)` admit a grouping into sizes `t`, `N − t` | the previous row; the converse by taking unions |\n| `IsRelax G S k` (def) and `isRelax_of_isBalancedBip` (L1′ b) | `IsBalancedBip G S k` | every component of `G.induce (univ \\ S)` has `card ≤ t` | `connectedComponent_subset_side`, `Finset.card_le_card` |\n| `not_isBalancedBip_threeEdges` (L1′ d) | the graph `3K₂` on `Fin 6`, `k = 0`, `S = ∅` | `IsRelax ∧ ¬ IsBalancedBip` | `decide` |\n| `subset_compl_closedNbhd_iff` (Galois step) | — | `R ⊆ univ \\ closedNbhd G A ↔ Disjoint A R ∧ ∀ u ∈ A, ∀ v ∈ R, ¬ G.Adj u v` | `mem_closedNbhd_iff`, `G.adj_symm` |\n| `closedNbhd_mono` | `A ⊆ A'` | `closedNbhd G A ⊆ closedNbhd G A'` | `Finset.biUnion_subset_biUnion_of_subset_left` |\n| `IsClosedPair G A R` (def) | — | `A = univ \\ closedNbhd G R ∧ R = univ \\ closedNbhd G A` | — |\n| `closure_subset` (L2 i) | `IsEmptyPair G A R` | `A ⊆ univ \\ closedNbhd G (univ \\ closedNbhd G A)` and `R ⊆ univ \\ closedNbhd G A` | `subset_compl_closedNbhd_iff` twice |\n| `closure_isClosedPair` (L2 ii–iii) | `IsEmptyPair G A R` | `IsClosedPair G (univ \\ closedNbhd G (univ \\ closedNbhd G A)) (univ \\ closedNbhd G A)` | `closure_subset`, `closedNbhd_mono`, `subset_compl_closedNbhd_iff` |\n| `sdiff_isClosedPair_eq_inter_boundary` (L2 iv) | `IsClosedPair G A R` | `univ \\ (A ∪ R) = (closedNbhd G A \\ A) ∩ (closedNbhd G R \\ R)` | `mem_closedNbhd_iff` |\n| `closure_card` (L2 v) | `IsEmptyPair G A R`, `t ≤ A.card`, `N − t ≤ R.card`, `(univ \\ (A ∪ R)).card ≤ k` | the same three bounds for the closed pair | `closure_subset`, `Finset.card_le_card`, `Finset.card_sdiff` |\n| `exists_closedPair_of_pathwidth_le` (L1 + L2) | `pathwidth G ≤ k`, `k < n` | `∃ A R, IsClosedPair G A R ∧ IsEmptyPair G A R ∧ t ≤ A.card ∧ N − t ≤ R.card ∧ (univ \\ (A ∪ R)).card ≤ k` | the two rows above and `exists_balanced_separator` |\n| `mem_boundary_coverGraph_iff` (L3 c) | `S : Fin m → Finset C` | `c ∈ closedNbhd (coverGraph S) A \\ A ↔ c ∉ A ∧ ∃ j, c ∈ S j ∧ ∃ a ∈ A, a ≠ c ∧ a ∈ S j` | `coverGraph`'s adjacency, `mem_closedNbhd_iff` |\n| `exists_balanced_separator_cover` (L3 b) | an instance with `mospValue ≤ k + 1`, `k < n` | `exists_balanced_separator` for `mospGraph`, with the empty pair stated as \"no pattern meets both\" | `MOSPGraph.lean`'s equality, `isEmptyPair_coverGraph_iff` |\n\nObstacle noted for item 10, not for this item: `isBalancedBip_iff_components`\nneeds connected components of an induced subgraph as `Finset`s with their\ncardinalities, which the development has not used so far (Mathlib's\n`SimpleGraph.ConnectedComponent` is a quotient type; `Fintype` instances\nexist, the union-of-supports bookkeeping does not). The pair form\n`IsBalancedBip` is what the probabilistic statement of item 08 needs, and\nit is cheap; the component form is for the LDP comparison and can be\nleft as a named `Prop` with this obstacle if it does not fit.\n\n### 2.7 Regenerate, size range, kill verdict, what this does not claim\n\n* **Regenerate.** `nice -n 19 python3 reports/scratch/separator_moment/02_lemmas/check.py --max-n 9 --workers 8`\n  (run with 4 workers on 2026-11-01 because the one-minute load was\n  above 24; the log says the wall time); `python3 -m pytest tests/test_separator_moment.py`.\n* **Size range.** Every graph on 1–9 vertices; 20,000 random `G(n, p)`\n  graphs at `n = 10, 11, 12` with `p ∈ {0.15, 0.3, 0.45, 0.6}` (seed = job\n  index); 2,000 random covers with 4–10 customers, `m ∈ {n, 2n}`, 2–4\n  customers per pattern (`generate_structured_instance(n, m, cpp, seed = index)`).\n  The lemmas are for every graph; the counts in the table are for these\n  sizes only.\n* **Kill verdict: not met.** Zero counterexamples to L1, L1′, L2 or L3 on\n  310,266 graphs. The item's premise — that a narrow layout forces a\n  balanced separator, a balanced empty bipartition and a closed pair at the\n  forced sizes — stands; the three structures exist on every graph checked\n  at `k = pw`, and at `k = pw − 1` they exist on about 45% of graphs at\n  7–9 vertices, so at these sizes the *necessary* condition is far from\n  sufficient — the slack the loop is about is already visible on nine\n  vertices, and it is the condition's, not only the union bound's.\n* **Does not claim.** Anything probabilistic: no rate, no constant, no\n  statement about `P_N(E_bip)`. That the closed pair is canonical (the\n  `A`-first and `R`-first fixpoints differ on about a tenth of the pairs\n  checked). That `E_relax` is a cheap relaxation at scale (it differs from\n  `E_bip` on under 2% of graphs at 9 vertices; the rate question is item\n  05's). That the share of graphs with a structure at `k = pw − 1` says\n  anything beyond nine vertices. The Lean rows of §2.6 are statements,\n  not proofs; item 10 proves them or names the obstacle.\nEOF\necho appended; tail -3 reports/scratch/separator_moment/02_lemmas/check.log | cut -c1-120","description":"Append the §2 prose to the report with a table placeholder"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":32,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3750,"cache_read_input_tokens":137119,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3750,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"79d5ae08-5614-4985-8469-5dab4f20e782","uuid":"bcf32320-4db3-4188-8275-d4cea069f743","timestamp":"2026-11-01T13:18:22.345Z","request_id":"req_011CgcGhuKp1zpyDG73N8P6L","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_011JhMCDxrLMDnm1ij21eGpE":{"command":"cd /home/al/dev/MOSP && cat >> reports/separator_moment.md <<'EOF'\n\n---\n\n## 2. The deterministic lemmas: balanced separator, its relaxation, the closed pair (item 02, 2026-11-01)\n\n**Question (A of the brief).** Exactly which structures does\n`pw(G) ≤ k < n` force, in a form a first moment can count once per\nseparator? Three, each one line from `exists_empty_pair_of_layout`\n(`EmptyPair.lean`), and each checked by brute force before being called a\nlemma: on every graph on 1–9 vertices from `nauty-geng` (288,266 graphs),\non 20,000 random `G(n, p)` graphs at `n = 10, 11, 12` and on 2,000 random\nclique covers at 4–10 customers (§2.4). **Zero counterexamples.** The\nkill criterion (a counterexample to L1, L1′ or L2) is **not met**.\n\nNotation, for the whole section: `G` is a graph on the vertex set `V`,\n`|V| = n`; `N[X] = X ∪ N(X)` is the closed neighbourhood and\n`N(X) = N[X] ∖ X` the *outer* boundary of `X`; a pair `(A, R)` of disjoint\nsets is **empty** when no edge joins `A` to `R`, equivalently\n`R ∩ N[A] = ∅`; `k` is an integer with `pw(G) ≤ k < n`,\n`N = n − k`, `t = ⌈N/2⌉`, so that `t ≥ N − t ≥ 0` and `t ≥ 1`. Nothing in\nthis section is probabilistic.\n\n### 2.1 L1 — the balanced separator at exact sizes\n\n**Lemma L1.** If `pw(G) ≤ k < n`, there are pairwise disjoint `A, R, S`\nwith `A ∪ R ∪ S = V`, `|A| = t`, `|R| = N − t`, `|S| = k` and no `A–R`\nedge.\n\n*Proof.* (Layout step) By `vertexSeparation_eq_pathwidth` there is a\nlayout `σ` with `vs(σ) = pw(G) ≤ k`. (Prefix step) Apply\n`exists_empty_pair_of_layout` at position `i = t − 1`, which is `< n`\nbecause `t ≤ N ≤ n`: the prefix `A` of size `t` and the inactive suffix\n`R₀` are disjoint, no edge joins them, and\n`n ≤ |A| + |R₀| + vs(σ) ≤ t + |R₀| + k`, i.e. `|R₀| ≥ N − t`\n(`card_inactiveSuffix`). (Shrink step) Take `R ⊆ R₀` with `|R| = N − t`;\nthe edge condition is universally quantified over `R₀`, so it holds on\n`R`, and `R ⊆ R₀` is disjoint from `A`. (Complement step)\n`S = V ∖ (A ∪ R)` has `n − t − (N − t) = k` vertices and is disjoint from\nboth. ∎\n\n*Remarks.* (i) The sizes are exact: `|R| = N − t = ⌊N/2⌋`, which is the\nitem's `n − t − k`. The Lean corollary `exists_empty_pair` rounds it to\n`|R| ≥ t − 1`, which coincides with `N − t` for `N` odd and is one less\nfor `N` even; the count on record (`FirstMoment.lean`,\n`card_emptyPairIndices`) uses `|R| = t − 1`, and plan §0 correction 2 is\nabout this rounding. L1 is the sharper form and is what §2.4 checks.\n(ii) `S` is the active suffix at position `t − 1` padded to exactly `k`\nvertices with vertices of `R₀`; which vertices are moved is immaterial,\nand the lemma says nothing about `S` beyond its size — that is what L2\nadds.\n\n### 2.2 L1′ — the separator events `E_bip` and `E_relax`\n\nFor a `k`-set `S ⊆ V` write `G − S` for the subgraph induced on\n`V ∖ S` (which has `N` vertices) and define two events:\n\n    E_bip(S):   the components of G − S can be grouped into two classes\n                whose vertex counts are t and N − t;\n    E_relax(S): no component of G − S has more than t vertices.\n\n**Lemma L1′.** (a) If `pw(G) ≤ k < n`, some `k`-set `S` satisfies\n`E_bip(S)`. (b) For every `k`-set `S`, `E_bip(S)` implies `E_relax(S)`.\n(c) For every `k`-set `S`, `E_bip(S)` holds iff `G` has an exact-size\nempty pair `(A, R)` (`|A| = t`, `|R| = N − t`, no `A–R` edge) with\n`V ∖ (A ∪ R) = S`. Hence *\"some `k`-set is in `E_bip`\"* and *\"an\nexact-size empty pair exists\"* are the same event; the two counts `X₂`\n(separators) and `X₁` (pairs) count it differently. (d) The inclusion in\n(b) is strict: on the graph `3K₂` (six vertices, edges `03, 14, 25`) with\n`k = 0`, `S = ∅` satisfies `E_relax` (components of sizes `2, 2, 2 ≤ t = 3`)\nand not `E_bip` (no sub-family of `{2, 2, 2}` sums to `3`). This is the\nfirst graph on six vertices in `nauty-geng` order with a `k`-set in\n`E_relax ∖ E_bip` (`check.log`, first line); its pathwidth is `1`, so at\n`k = pw` the two events agree on it.\n\n*Proof.* (a) Take `A, R, S` from L1. (Connectivity step) A component `C`\nof `G − S` is connected and lies in `A ∪ R`; if it met both `A` and `R`,\nsome edge of `C` — a path inside `C` from a vertex of `A` to a vertex of\n`R` has a first `A–R` edge — would join `A` to `R`, contradicting\nemptiness. So `C ⊆ A` or `C ⊆ R`. (Grouping step) The components inside\n`A` partition `A` and sum to `t`; those inside `R` sum to `N − t`.\n(b) Each component lies in a class of size `t` or `N − t ≤ t`.\n(c, ⇒) Let `A` be the union of the class of size `t` and `R` the union\nof the other; they partition `V ∖ S`, have the right sizes, and an edge\nof `G − S` joins two vertices of one component, hence of one class, so\nno edge joins `A` to `R` (an `A–R` edge of `G` would have both ends\noutside `S`, so it would be an edge of `G − S`). (c, ⇐) is (a)'s\nargument applied to the given pair. (d) is checked directly. ∎\n\n*Remark.* `E_relax` is the Lean-cheap and LDP-shaped form (plan §0,\ncorrection 3; §1.6): it is a statement about component sizes only, and\n`P_N(E_relax)` on `G(N, p)` is the lower tail of the largest component.\n`E_bip` is the event the pair count actually witnesses, and items 05–06\ncompute `P_N(E_bip)` from the full component-size partition, not from\nthe largest component alone. On the graphs checked the two differ at\n`k = pw` on `0.3%` of graphs at 7 vertices and `1.9%` at 9 (§2.4,\ncolumn \"`E_relax ⊋ E_bip`\"), so at these sizes the relaxation is nearly\nfree; nothing here says what it costs at the scale of the rate.\n\n### 2.3 L2 — the closed pair, by the alternating closure\n\n**Lemma L2.** Let `(A, R)` be an empty pair with `|A| ≥ t`,\n`|R| ≥ N − t` and `|V ∖ (A ∪ R)| ≤ k`. Put\n\n    R* = V ∖ N[A],      A* = V ∖ N[R*],      S* = V ∖ (A* ∪ R*).\n\nThen: (i) `A ⊆ A*` and `R ⊆ R*` (monotone); (ii) `(A*, R*)` is an empty\npair; (iii) `R* = V ∖ N[A*]` and `A* = V ∖ N[R*]` (a **closed pair**), so\nthe alternating closure `R₁ = V ∖ N[A]`, `A₁ = V ∖ N[R₁]`, `R₂ = V ∖ N[A₁]`,\n`A₂ = V ∖ N[R₂]`, … is constant from `(A₁, R₁) = (A*, R*)` on — it stops\nafter **one round**, not after an unbounded iteration; (iv)\n`S* = N(A*) ∩ N(R*)`; (v) `|A*| ≥ t`, `|R*| ≥ N − t`, `|S*| ≤ k`.\nConversely, every closed pair `(A, R)` (one with `A = V ∖ N[R]` and\n`R = V ∖ N[A]`) is empty with `V ∖ (A ∪ R) = N(A) ∩ N(R)`, and is\ndetermined by either side.\n\n*Proof.* (Galois step) For any `X, Y ⊆ V`: `Y ⊆ V ∖ N[X]` iff `Y ∩ X = ∅`\nand no edge joins `X` to `Y`, iff `X ⊆ V ∖ N[Y]`, by the symmetry of\nadjacency; and `X ⊆ X′` implies `N[X] ⊆ N[X′]` (monotone step).\n(i) `(A, R)` is empty, so `R ⊆ V ∖ N[A] = R*` by the Galois step.\n`R* ∩ N[A] = ∅` by definition, so `(A, R*)` is empty and the Galois step\ngives `A ⊆ V ∖ N[R*] = A*`. (ii) `A* ∩ N[R*] = ∅` by definition.\n(iii) From (ii) and the Galois step, `R* ⊆ V ∖ N[A*]`; from `A ⊆ A*` and\nthe monotone step, `V ∖ N[A*] ⊆ V ∖ N[A] = R*`. So `R* = V ∖ N[A*]`, and\n`A* = V ∖ N[R*]` is the definition. Hence `R₂ = V ∖ N[A₁] = R₁` and\n`A₂ = V ∖ N[R₂] = A₁`, and by induction every later term equals these.\n(iv) `v ∈ S*` iff `v ∉ A*` and `v ∉ R*`; `v ∉ A* = V ∖ N[R*]` means\n`v ∈ N[R*]`, which with `v ∉ R*` is `v ∈ N(R*)`; symmetrically\n`v ∈ N(A*)`. Conversely a vertex of `N(A*) ∩ N(R*)` is outside `A*` and\noutside `R*` by the definition of the outer boundary. (v) From (i),\n`|A*| ≥ |A| ≥ t` and `|R*| ≥ |R| ≥ N − t`; then\n`|S*| = n − |A*| − |R*| ≤ n − t − (N − t) = k`. The converse is (ii)–(iv)\nread with `A* = A`, `R* = R`; \"determined by either side\" is\n`R = V ∖ N[A]`. ∎\n\n*Remarks.* (i) **The fixpoint is a representative, not the\nrepresentative.** Closing `R` first gives `(V ∖ N[R], V ∖ N[V ∖ N[R]])`,\nwhich can differ from `(A*, R*)`: on the graphs checked the two maps\ndisagree on `10%` of exact-size pairs at 7–9 vertices (§2.4, column\n\"closures differ\"). A union bound over closed pairs needs only *a*\nmap from exact-size pairs to closed pairs of the forced sizes, and L2\ngives one (`A` first; the mirror is another); it does not need the map\nto be canonical, and this document never says it is. (ii) The item\nexpected an iteration `A ⊆ A₁ ⊆ A₂ ⊆ …`; (iii) says the chain is\n`A ⊆ A₁ = A₂ = …`, `R ⊆ R₁ = R₂ = …`. Monotonicity holds as asserted\n(with equality from the second step), which is what the check verifies\nat every step — the item's expectation is corrected, not contradicted.\n(iii) Because every closed pair is determined by `A`, the closed pairs\nof the forced sizes (`X₃` below) are enumerated exactly by running over\nall `A ⊆ V`, setting `R = V ∖ N[A]` and testing `A = V ∖ N[R]` and the\nsizes; no pair is reached twice. (iv) The image of the exact-size pairs\nunder the `A`-first closure (`X₃′`) can be a proper subset of `X₃`: a\nclosed pair of the forced sizes contains exact-size sub-pairs, but\nclosing a sub-pair may land on a *larger* closed pair (its `V ∖ N[A′]`\ncontains `V ∖ N[A]`). Both counts are valid first moments; item 03\nmeasures both.\n\n### 2.4 The check, before any of the above was called a lemma\n\n`reports/scratch/separator_moment/02_lemmas/check.py` (log beside it),\non the functions of `learning/separator_moment.py` (tested in\n`tests/test_separator_moment.py`, which also re-enumerates every\nlabelled graph to 6 vertices and pins the subset DP to\n`learning.first_moment.vertex_separation_brute` and\n`fixed_parameter_algorithm.pathwidth.compute_pathwidth`). For each graph,\n`k = pw(G)` by the subset DP; then\n\n* **L1**: `X₁ > 0`, where `X₁` is the number of ordered exact-size empty\n  pairs (`|A| = t`, `|R| = N − t`; for `N` even `(A, R)` and `(R, A)` are\n  both counted);\n* **L1′**: `X₂ > 0`, `X₂ ≤ X₂′`, and `X₁ > 0 ⟺ X₂ > 0`, where `X₂`, `X₂′`\n  are the numbers of `k`-sets `S` in `E_bip` and `E_relax`, computed from\n  the components of `G − S` and a subset-sum over their sizes;\n* **L2**: from every exact-size pair (all of them when `X₁ ≤ 200`, the\n  first 200 otherwise), the closure sequence is monotone at every step,\n  the second round repeats the first, the fixpoint is closed, empty, of\n  the forced sizes, with `S* = N(A*) ∩ N(R*)`, and lies in `X₃` (all\n  closed pairs of the forced sizes, by the enumeration of remark (iii));\n  the share of pairs whose `A`-first and `R`-first fixpoints differ;\n* **L3** (covers only): a pair is empty iff no pattern meets both sides\n  (on every exact-size pair and on 20 random disjoint pairs per\n  instance), and every customer of `S*` shares a pattern with `A*` and\n  with `R*`;\n* at `k = pw − 1`: whether an exact-size pair (equivalently a `k`-set in\n  `E_bip`) and whether a `k`-set in `E_relax` still exist — the necessary\n  condition's slack at these sizes.\n\nTABLE_PLACEHOLDER\n\n### 2.5 L3 — the MOSP forms\n\nLet the instance have customers `C` (`|C| = n`) and patterns\n`S_1, …, S_m ⊆ C`; its MOSP graph `mospGraph` (`MOSPGraph.lean`;\n`coverGraph` in `CliqueCoverMoment.lean`) joins two distinct customers\niff some pattern contains both, and `mospValue = pw(mospGraph) + 1`\n(`MOSPGraph.lean`, both directions). With `k = mospValue − 1 < n`:\n\n**Lemma L3.** (a) For disjoint `A, R ⊆ C`, `(A, R)` is an empty pair of\n`mospGraph` iff no pattern meets both `A` and `R`\n(`isEmptyPair_coverGraph_iff`). (b) L1, L1′ and L2 hold for `mospGraph`\nwith `S` a set of `k` customers; in particular every instance with\n`mospValue ≤ k + 1` has `k` customers whose removal leaves customer\nclasses of sizes `t` and `N − t` such that no pattern meets both classes.\n(c) In a closed pair `(A*, R*)` of `mospGraph`, a customer `c` lies in\n`S* = N(A*) ∩ N(R*)` iff `c ∉ A* ∪ R*`, some pattern contains `c` and a\ncustomer of `A*`, and some pattern contains `c` and a customer of `R*`.\n\n*Proof.* (a) is the cited lemma. (b) is L1, L1′, L2 applied to the graph\n`mospGraph`, with `k ≥ pw` given by the equality theorem. (c) `c ∈ N(X)`\nfor `mospGraph` means `c ∉ X` and `c` is adjacent to some `x ∈ X`, i.e.\nsome pattern contains both `c` and `x`; apply L2 (iv). ∎\n\nThe check of §2.4 runs L1, L1′, L2 on the MOSP graphs of 2,000 random\ncovers and tests (a) and (c) directly against the matrix.\n\n### 2.6 Lean-ready statements for item 10\n\nAll over `{V : Type*} [Fintype V] [DecidableEq V] (G : SimpleGraph V)\n[DecidableRel G.Adj]`, in the vocabulary of `EmptyPair.lean`\n(`closedNbhd`, `IsEmptyPair` from `FirstMoment.lean`), with `n = Fintype.card V`,\n`t = (n − k + 1) / 2`, `N − t = (n − k) / 2` in `ℕ` arithmetic.\n\n| name | hypotheses | conclusion | rests on |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `exists_balanced_separator` (L1) | `pathwidth G ≤ k`, `k < n` | `∃ A R S : Finset V`, pairwise disjoint, `A ∪ R ∪ S = univ`, `A.card = t`, `R.card = N − t`, `S.card = k`, `∀ u ∈ A, ∀ v ∈ R, ¬ G.Adj u v` | `vertexSeparation_eq_pathwidth`, `exists_empty_pair_of_layout` at `i = t − 1`, `Finset.exists_subset_card_eq`, `Finset.card_sdiff` |\n| `IsBalancedBip G S k` (def) | — | `∃ A R, Disjoint A R ∧ A ∪ R = univ \\ S ∧ A.card = t ∧ R.card = N − t ∧ IsEmptyPair G A R` — the pair form of `E_bip`, which L1′ (c) shows equivalent to the component form | — |\n| `exists_isBalancedBip` (L1′ a) | `pathwidth G ≤ k`, `k < n` | `∃ S, S.card = k ∧ IsBalancedBip G S k` | `exists_balanced_separator` |\n| `connectedComponent_subset_side` (connectivity step) | `IsEmptyPair G A R`, `C` a connected component of `G.induce (A ∪ R)` | `C ⊆ A ∨ C ⊆ R` | induction on `SimpleGraph.Walk` in the induced graph |\n| `isBalancedBip_iff_components` (L1′ c) | `S.card = k` | `IsBalancedBip G S k ↔` the components of `G.induce (univ \\ S)` admit a grouping into sizes `t`, `N − t` | the previous row; the converse by taking unions |\n| `IsRelax G S k` (def) and `isRelax_of_isBalancedBip` (L1′ b) | `IsBalancedBip G S k` | every component of `G.induce (univ \\ S)` has `card ≤ t` | `connectedComponent_subset_side`, `Finset.card_le_card` |\n| `not_isBalancedBip_threeEdges` (L1′ d) | the graph `3K₂` on `Fin 6`, `k = 0`, `S = ∅` | `IsRelax ∧ ¬ IsBalancedBip` | `decide` |\n| `subset_compl_closedNbhd_iff` (Galois step) | — | `R ⊆ univ \\ closedNbhd G A ↔ Disjoint A R ∧ ∀ u ∈ A, ∀ v ∈ R, ¬ G.Adj u v` | `mem_closedNbhd_iff`, `G.adj_symm` |\n| `closedNbhd_mono` | `A ⊆ A'` | `closedNbhd G A ⊆ closedNbhd G A'` | `Finset.biUnion_subset_biUnion_of_subset_left` |\n| `IsClosedPair G A R` (def) | — | `A = univ \\ closedNbhd G R ∧ R = univ \\ closedNbhd G A` | — |\n| `closure_subset` (L2 i) | `IsEmptyPair G A R` | `A ⊆ univ \\ closedNbhd G (univ \\ closedNbhd G A)` and `R ⊆ univ \\ closedNbhd G A` | `subset_compl_closedNbhd_iff` twice |\n| `closure_isClosedPair` (L2 ii–iii) | `IsEmptyPair G A R` | `IsClosedPair G (univ \\ closedNbhd G (univ \\ closedNbhd G A)) (univ \\ closedNbhd G A)` | `closure_subset`, `closedNbhd_mono`, `subset_compl_closedNbhd_iff` |\n| `sdiff_isClosedPair_eq_inter_boundary` (L2 iv) | `IsClosedPair G A R` | `univ \\ (A ∪ R) = (closedNbhd G A \\ A) ∩ (closedNbhd G R \\ R)` | `mem_closedNbhd_iff` |\n| `closure_card` (L2 v) | `IsEmptyPair G A R`, `t ≤ A.card`, `N − t ≤ R.card`, `(univ \\ (A ∪ R)).card ≤ k` | the same three bounds for the closed pair | `closure_subset`, `Finset.card_le_card`, `Finset.card_sdiff` |\n| `exists_closedPair_of_pathwidth_le` (L1 + L2) | `pathwidth G ≤ k`, `k < n` | `∃ A R, IsClosedPair G A R ∧ IsEmptyPair G A R ∧ t ≤ A.card ∧ N − t ≤ R.card ∧ (univ \\ (A ∪ R)).card ≤ k` | the two rows above and `exists_balanced_separator` |\n| `mem_boundary_coverGraph_iff` (L3 c) | `S : Fin m → Finset C` | `c ∈ closedNbhd (coverGraph S) A \\ A ↔ c ∉ A ∧ ∃ j, c ∈ S j ∧ ∃ a ∈ A, a ≠ c ∧ a ∈ S j` | `coverGraph`'s adjacency, `mem_closedNbhd_iff` |\n| `exists_balanced_separator_cover` (L3 b) | an instance with `mospValue ≤ k + 1`, `k < n` | `exists_balanced_separator` for `mospGraph`, with the empty pair stated as \"no pattern meets both\" | `MOSPGraph.lean`'s equality, `isEmptyPair_coverGraph_iff` |\n\nObstacle noted for item 10, not for this item: `isBalancedBip_iff_components`\nneeds connected components of an induced subgraph as `Finset`s with their\ncardinalities, which the development has not used so far (Mathlib's\n`SimpleGraph.ConnectedComponent` is a quotient type; `Fintype` instances\nexist, the union-of-supports bookkeeping does not). The pair form\n`IsBalancedBip` is what the probabilistic statement of item 08 needs, and\nit is cheap; the component form is for the LDP comparison and can be\nleft as a named `Prop` with this obstacle if it does not fit.\n\n### 2.7 Regenerate, size range, kill verdict, what this does not claim\n\n* **Regenerate.** `nice -n 19 python3 reports/scratch/separator_moment/02_lemmas/check.py --max-n 9 --workers 8`\n  (run with 4 workers on 2026-11-01 because the one-minute load was\n  above 24; the log says the wall time); `python3 -m pytest tests/test_separator_moment.py`.\n* **Size range.** Every graph on 1–9 vertices; 20,000 random `G(n, p)`\n  graphs at `n = 10, 11, 12` with `p ∈ {0.15, 0.3, 0.45, 0.6}` (seed = job\n  index); 2,000 random covers with 4–10 customers, `m ∈ {n, 2n}`, 2–4\n  customers per pattern (`generate_structured_instance(n, m, cpp, seed = index)`).\n  The lemmas are for every graph; the counts in the table are for these\n  sizes only.\n* **Kill verdict: not met.** Zero counterexamples to L1, L1′, L2 or L3 on\n  310,266 graphs. The item's premise — that a narrow layout forces a\n  balanced separator, a balanced empty bipartition and a closed pair at the\n  forced sizes — stands; the three structures exist on every graph checked\n  at `k = pw`, and at `k = pw − 1` they exist on about 45% of graphs at\n  7–9 vertices, so at these sizes the *necessary* condition is far from\n  sufficient — the slack the loop is about is already visible on nine\n  vertices, and it is the condition's, not only the union bound's.\n* **Does not claim.** Anything probabilistic: no rate, no constant, no\n  statement about `P_N(E_bip)`. That the closed pair is canonical (the\n  `A`-first and `R`-first fixpoints differ on about a tenth of the pairs\n  checked). That `E_relax` is a cheap relaxation at scale (it differs from\n  `E_bip` on under 2% of graphs at 9 vertices; the rate question is item\n  05's). That the share of graphs with a structure at `k = pw − 1` says\n  anything beyond nine vertices. The Lean rows of §2.6 are statements,\n  not proofs; item 10 proves them or names the obstacle.\nEOF\necho appended; tail -3 reports/scratch/separator_moment/02_lemmas/check.log | cut -c1-120","description":"Append the §2 prose to the report with a table placeholder"}},"wire_ingest_context":{"toolu_011JhMCDxrLMDnm1ij21eGpE":{"cwd":"/home/al/dev/MOSP"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011JhMCDxrLMDnm1ij21eGpE","type":"tool_result","content":"appended\n{\"set\": \"random n=12\", \"graphs\": 6666, \"violations\": 0, \"viol_kinds\": {}, \"X1_med\": 16.0, \"X1_max\": 3192, \"X2_med\": 9.0,\n{\"set\": \"covers n<=10\", \"graphs\": 2000, \"violations\": 0, \"viol_kinds\": {}, \"X1_med\": 5.0, \"X1_max\": 74, \"X2_med\": 4.0, \"\nTOTAL graphs=310266 violations=0 wall=113s","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"79d5ae08-5614-4985-8469-5dab4f20e782","uuid":"50930a49-e84f-4a58-841e-9fb47f9ab62e","timestamp":"2026-11-01T13:18:22.514Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"appended\n{\"set\": \"random n=12\", \"graphs\": 6666, \"violations\": 0, \"viol_kinds\": {}, \"X1_med\": 16.0, \"X1_max\": 3192, \"X2_med\": 9.0,\n{\"set\": \"covers n<=10\", \"graphs\": 2000, \"violations\": 0, \"viol_kinds\": {}, \"X1_med\": 5.0, \"X1_max\": 74, \"X2_med\": 4.0, \"\nTOTAL graphs=310266 violations=0 wall=113s","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false,"bashEditDiff":{"files":[{"filePath":"/home/al/dev/MOSP/reports/separator_moment.md","hunks":[{"oldStart":358,"oldLines":3,"newStart":358,"newLines":280,"lines":[" - The fractions of a gap in §1.4 use the best *upper bounds* where the","   truth is unknown (edge expansion at small `Δ`, cubic bisection), so they","   are lower bounds on the fraction of the true gap.","+","+---","+","+## 2. The deterministic lemmas: balanced separator, its relaxation, the closed pair (item 02, 2026-11-01)","+","+**Question (A of the brief).** Exactly which structures does","+`pw(G) ≤ k < n` force, in a form a first moment can count once per","+separator? Three, each one line from `exists_empty_pair_of_layout`","+(`EmptyPair.lean`), and each checked by brute force before being called a","+lemma: on every graph on 1–9 vertices from `nauty-geng` (288,266 graphs),","+on 20,000 random `G(n, p)` graphs at `n = 10, 11, 12` and on 2,000 random","+clique covers at 4–10 customers (§2.4). **Zero counterexamples.** The","+kill criterion (a counterexample to L1, L1′ or L2) is **not met**.","+","+Notation, for the whole section: `G` is a graph on the vertex set `V`,","+`|V| = n`; `N[X] = X ∪ N(X)` is the closed neighbourhood and","+`N(X) = N[X] ∖ X` the *outer* boundary of `X`; a pair `(A, R)` of disjoint","+sets is **empty** when no edge joins `A` to `R`, equivalently","+`R ∩ N[A] = ∅`; `k` is an integer with `pw(G) ≤ k < n`,","+`N = n − k`, `t = ⌈N/2⌉`, so that `t ≥ N − t ≥ 0` and `t ≥ 1`. Nothing in","+this section is probabilistic.","+","+### 2.1 L1 — the balanced separator at exact sizes","+","+**Lemma L1.** If `pw(G) ≤ k < n`, there are pairwise disjoint `A, R, S`","+with `A ∪ R ∪ S = V`, `|A| = t`, `|R| = N − t`, `|S| = k` and no `A–R`","+edge.","+","+*Proof.* (Layout step) By `vertexSeparation_eq_pathwidth` there is a","+layout `σ` with `vs(σ) = pw(G) ≤ k`. (Prefix step) Apply","+`exists_empty_pair_of_layout` at position `i = t − 1`, which is `< n`","+because `t ≤ N ≤ n`: the prefix `A` of size `t` and the inactive suffix","+`R₀` are disjoint, no edge joins them, and","+`n ≤ |A| + |R₀| + vs(σ) ≤ t + |R₀| + k`, i.e. `|R₀| ≥ N − t`","+(`card_inactiveSuffix`). (Shrink step) Take `R ⊆ R₀` with `|R| = N − t`;","+the edge condition is universally quantified over `R₀`, so it holds on","+`R`, and `R ⊆ R₀` is disjoint from `A`. (Complement step)","+`S = V ∖ (A ∪ R)` has `n − t − (N − t) = k` vertices and is disjoint from","+both. ∎","+","+*Remarks.* (i) The sizes are exact: `|R| = N − t = ⌊N/2⌋`, which is the","+item's `n − t − k`. The Lean corollary `exists_empty_pair` rounds it to","+`|R| ≥ t − 1`, which coincides with `N − t` for `N` odd and is one less","+for `N` even; the count on record (`FirstMoment.lean`,","+`card_emptyPairIndices`) uses `|R| = t − 1`, and plan §0 correction 2 is","+about this rounding. L1 is the sharper form and is what §2.4 checks.","+(ii) `S` is the active suffix at position `t − 1` padded to exactly `k`","+vertices with vertices of `R₀`; which vertices are moved is immaterial,","+and the lemma says nothing about `S` beyond its size — that is what L2","+adds.","+","+### 2.2 L1′ — the separator events `E_bip` and `E_relax`","+","+For a `k`-set `S ⊆ V` write `G − S` for the subgraph induced on","+`V ∖ S` (which has `N` vertices) and define two events:","+","+    E_bip(S):   the components of G − S can be grouped into two classes","+                whose vertex counts are t and N − t;","+    E_relax(S): no component of G − S has more than t vertices.","+","+**Lemma L1′.** (a) If `pw(G) ≤ k < n`, some `k`-set `S` satisfies","+`E_bip(S)`. (b) For every `k`-set `S`, `E_bip(S)` implies `E_relax(S)`.","+(c) For every `k`-set `S`, `E_bip(S)` holds iff `G` has an exact-size","+empty pair `(A, R)` (`|A| = t`, `|R| = N − t`, no `A–R` edge) with","+`V ∖ (A ∪ R) = S`. Hence *\"some `k`-set is in `E_bip`\"* and *\"an","+exact-size empty pair exists\"* are the same event; the two counts `X₂`","+(separators) and `X₁` (pairs) count it differently. (d) The inclusion in","+(b) is strict: on the graph `3K₂` (six vertices, edges `03, 14, 25`) with","+`k = 0`, `S = ∅` satisfies `E_relax` (components of sizes `2, 2, 2 ≤ t = 3`)","+and not `E_bip` (no sub-family of `{2, 2, 2}` sums to `3`). This is the","+first graph on six vertices in `nauty-geng` order with a `k`-set in","+`E_relax ∖ E_bip` (`check.log`, first line); its pathwidth is `1`, so at","+`k = pw` the two events agree on it.","+","+*Proof.* (a) Take `A, R, S` from L1. (Connectivity step) A component `C`","+of `G − S` is connected and lies in `A ∪ R`; if it met both `A` and `R`,","+some edge of `C` — a path inside `C` from a vertex of `A` to a vertex of","+`R` has a first `A–R` edge — would join `A` to `R`, contradicting","+emptiness. So `C ⊆ A` or `C ⊆ R`. (Grouping step) The components inside","+`A` partition `A` and sum to `t`; those inside `R` sum to `N − t`.","+(b) Each component lies in a class of size `t` or `N − t ≤ t`.","+(c, ⇒) Let `A` be the union of the class of size `t` and `R` the union","+of the other; they partition `V ∖ S`, have the right sizes, and an edge","+of `G − S` joins two vertices of one component, hence of one class, so","+no edge joins `A` to `R` (an `A–R` edge of `G` would have both ends","+outside `S`, so it would be an edge of `G − S`). (c, ⇐) is (a)'s","+argument applied to the given pair. (d) is checked directly. ∎","+","+*Remark.* `E_relax` is the Lean-cheap and LDP-shaped form (plan §0,","+correction 3; §1.6): it is a statement about component sizes only, and","+`P_N(E_relax)` on `G(N, p)` is the lower tail of the largest component.","+`E_bip` is the event the pair count actually witnesses, and items 05–06","+compute `P_N(E_bip)` from the full component-size partition, not from","+the largest component alone. On the graphs checked the two differ at","+`k = pw` on `0.3%` of graphs at 7 vertices and `1.9%` at 9 (§2.4,","+column \"`E_relax ⊋ E_bip`\"), so at these sizes the relaxation is nearly","+free; nothing here says what it costs at the scale of the rate.","+","+### 2.3 L2 — the closed pair, by the alternating closure","+","+**Lemma L2.** Let `(A, R)` be an empty pair with `|A| ≥ t`,","+`|R| ≥ N − t` and `|V ∖ (A ∪ R)| ≤ k`. Put","+","+    R* = V ∖ N[A],      A* = V ∖ N[R*],      S* = V ∖ (A* ∪ R*).","+","+Then: (i) `A ⊆ A*` and `R ⊆ R*` (monotone); (ii) `(A*, R*)` is an empty","+pair; (iii) `R* = V ∖ N[A*]` and `A* = V ∖ N[R*]` (a **closed pair**), so","+the alternating closure `R₁ = V ∖ N[A]`, `A₁ = V ∖ N[R₁]`, `R₂ = V ∖ N[A₁]`,","+`A₂ = V ∖ N[R₂]`, … is constant from `(A₁, R₁) = (A*, R*)` on — it stops","+after **one round**, not after an unbounded iteration; (iv)","+`S* = N(A*) ∩ N(R*)`; (v) `|A*| ≥ t`, `|R*| ≥ N − t`, `|S*| ≤ k`.","+Conversely, every closed pair `(A, R)` (one with `A = V ∖ N[R]` and","+`R = V ∖ N[A]`) is empty with `V ∖ (A ∪ R) = N(A) ∩ N(R)`, and is","+determined by either side.","+","+*Proof.* (Galois step) For any `X, Y ⊆ V`: `Y ⊆ V ∖ N[X]` iff `Y ∩ X = ∅`","+and no edge joins `X` to `Y`, iff `X ⊆ V ∖ N[Y]`, by the symmetry of","+adjacency; and `X ⊆ X′` implies `N[X] ⊆ N[X′]` (monotone step).","+(i) `(A, R)` is empty, so `R ⊆ V ∖ N[A] = R*` by the Galois step.","+`R* ∩ N[A] = ∅` by definition, so `(A, R*)` is empty and the Galois step","+gives `A ⊆ V ∖ N[R*] = A*`. (ii) `A* ∩ N[R*] = ∅` by definition.","+(iii) From (ii) and the Galois step, `R* ⊆ V ∖ N[A*]`; from `A ⊆ A*` and","+the monotone step, `V ∖ N[A*] ⊆ V ∖ N[A] = R*`. So `R* = V ∖ N[A*]`, and","+`A* = V ∖ N[R*]` is the definition. Hence `R₂ = V ∖ N[A₁] = R₁` and","+`A₂ = V ∖ N[R₂] = A₁`, and by induction every later term equals these.","+(iv) `v ∈ S*` iff `v ∉ A*` and `v ∉ R*`; `v ∉ A* = V ∖ N[R*]` means","+`v ∈ N[R*]`, which with `v ∉ R*` is `v ∈ N(R*)`; symmetrically","+`v ∈ N(A*)`. Conversely a vertex of `N(A*) ∩ N(R*)` is outside `A*` and","+outside `R*` by the definition of the outer boundary. (v) From (i),","+`|A*| ≥ |A| ≥ t` and `|R*| ≥ |R| ≥ N − t`; then","+`|S*| = n − |A*| − |R*| ≤ n − t − (N − t) = k`. The converse is (ii)–(iv)","+read with `A* = A`, `R* = R`; \"determined by either side\" is","+`R = V ∖ N[A]`. ∎","+","+*Remarks.* (i) **The fixpoint is a representative, not the","+representative.** Closing `R` first gives `(V ∖ N[R], V ∖ N[V ∖ N[R]])`,","+which can differ from `(A*, R*)`: on the graphs checked the two maps","+disagree on `10%` of exact-size pairs at 7–9 vertices (§2.4, column","+\"closures differ\"). A union bound over closed pairs needs only *a*","+map from exact-size pairs to closed pairs of the forced sizes, and L2","+gives one (`A` first; the mirror is another); it does not need the map","+to be canonical, and this document never says it is. (ii) The item","+expected an iteration `A ⊆ A₁ ⊆ A₂ ⊆ …`; (iii) says the chain is","+`A ⊆ A₁ = A₂ = …`, `R ⊆ R₁ = R₂ = …`. Monotonicity holds as asserted","+(with equality from the second step), which is what the check verifies","+at every step — the item's expectation is corrected, not contradicted.","+(iii) Because every closed pair is determined by `A`, the closed pairs","+of the forced sizes (`X₃` below) are enumerated exactly by running over","+all `A ⊆ V`, setting `R = V ∖ N[A]` and testing `A = V ∖ N[R]` and the","+sizes; no pair is reached twice. (iv) The image of the exact-size pairs","+under the `A`-first closure (`X₃′`) can be a proper subset of `X₃`: a","+closed pair of the forced sizes contains exact-size sub-pairs, but","+closing a sub-pair may land on a *larger* closed pair (its `V ∖ N[A′]`","+contains `V ∖ N[A]`). Both counts are valid first moments; item 03","+measures both.","+","+### 2.4 The check, before any of the above was called a lemma","+","+`reports/scratch/separator_moment/02_lemmas/check.py` (log beside it),","+on the functions of `learning/separator_moment.py` (tested in","+`tests/test_separator_moment.py`, which also re-enumerates every","+labelled graph to 6 vertices and pins the subset DP to","+`learning.first_moment.vertex_separation_brute` and","+`fixed_parameter_algorithm.pathwidth.compute_pathwidth`). For each graph,","+`k = pw(G)` by the subset DP; then","+","+* **L1**: `X₁ > 0`, where `X₁` is the number of ordered exact-size empty","+  pairs (`|A| = t`, `|R| = N − t`; for `N` even `(A, R)` and `(R, A)` are","+  both counted);","+* **L1′**: `X₂ > 0`, `X₂ ≤ X₂′`, and `X₁ > 0 ⟺ X₂ > 0`, where `X₂`, `X₂′`","+  are the numbers of `k`-sets `S` in `E_bip` and `E_relax`, computed from","+  the components of `G − S` and a subset-sum over their sizes;","+* **L2**: from every exact-size pair (all of them when `X₁ ≤ 200`, the","+  first 200 otherwise), the closure sequence is monotone at every step,","+  the second round repeats the first, the fixpoint is closed, empty, of","+  the forced sizes, with `S* = N(A*) ∩ N(R*)`, and lies in `X₃` (all","+  closed pairs of the forced sizes, by the enumeration of remark (iii));","+  the share of pairs whose `A`-first and `R`-first fixpoints differ;","+* **L3** (covers only): a pair is empty iff no pattern meets both sides","+  (on every exact-size pair and on 20 random disjoint pairs per","+  instance), and every customer of `S*` shares a pattern with `A*` and","+  with `R*`;","+* at `k = pw − 1`: whether an exact-size pair (equivalently a `k`-set in","+  `E_bip`) and whether a `k`-set in `E_relax` still exist — the necessary","+  condition's slack at these sizes.","+","+TABLE_PLACEHOLDER","+","+### 2.5 L3 — the MOSP forms","+","+Let the instance have customers `C` (`|C| = n`) and patterns","+`S_1, …, S_m ⊆ C`; its MOSP graph `mospGraph` (`MOSPGraph.lean`;","+`coverGraph` in `CliqueCoverMoment.lean`) joins two distinct customers","+iff some pattern contains both, and `mospValue = pw(mospGraph) + 1`","+(`MOSPGraph.lean`, both directions). With `k = mospValue − 1 < n`:","+","+**Lemma L3.** (a) For disjoint `A, R ⊆ C`, `(A, R)` is an empty pair of","+`mospGraph` iff no pattern meets both `A` and `R`","+(`isEmptyPair_coverGraph_iff`). (b) L1, L1′ and L2 hold for `mospGraph`","+with `S` a set of `k` customers; in particular every instance with","+`mospValue ≤ k + 1` has `k` customers whose removal leaves customer","+classes of sizes `t` and `N − t` such that no pattern meets both classes.","+(c) In a closed pair `(A*, R*)` of `mospGraph`, a customer `c` lies in","+`S* = N(A*) ∩ N(R*)` iff `c ∉ A* ∪ R*`, some pattern contains `c` and a","+customer of `A*`, and some pattern contains `c` and a customer of `R*`.","+","+*Proof.* (a) is the cited lemma. (b) is L1, L1′, L2 applied to the graph","+`mospGraph`, with `k ≥ pw` given by the equality theorem. (c) `c ∈ N(X)`","+for `mospGraph` means `c ∉ X` and `c` is adjacent to some `x ∈ X`, i.e.","+some pattern contains both `c` and `x`; apply L2 (iv). ∎","+","+The check of §2.4 runs L1, L1′, L2 on the MOSP graphs of 2,000 random","+covers and tests (a) and (c) directly against the matrix.","+","+### 2.6 Lean-ready statements for item 10","+","+All over `{V : Type*} [Fintype V] [DecidableEq V] (G : SimpleGraph V)","+[DecidableRel G.Adj]`, in the vocabulary of `EmptyPair.lean`","+(`closedNbhd`, `IsEmptyPair` from `FirstMoment.lean`), with `n = Fintype.card V`,","+`t = (n − k + 1) / 2`, `N − t = (n − k) / 2` in `ℕ` arithmetic.","+","+| name | hypotheses | conclusion | rests on |","+|---|---|---|---|","+| `exists_balanced_separator` (L1) | `pathwidth G ≤ k`, `k < n` | `∃ A R S : Finset V`, pairwise disjoint, `A ∪ R ∪ S = univ`, `A.card = t`, `R.card = N − t`, `S.card = k`, `∀ u ∈ A, ∀ v ∈ R, ¬ G.Adj u v` | `vertexSeparation_eq_pathwidth`, `exists_empty_pair_of_layout` at `i = t − 1`, `Finset.exists_subset_card_eq`, `Finset.card_sdiff` |","+| `IsBalancedBip G S k` (def) | — | `∃ A R, Disjoint A R ∧ A ∪ R = univ \\ S ∧ A.card = t ∧ R.card = N − t ∧ IsEmptyPair G A R` — the pair form of `E_bip`, which L1′ (c) shows equivalent to the component form | — |","+| `exists_isBalancedBip` (L1′ a) | `pathwidth G ≤ k`, `k < n` | `∃ S, S.card = k ∧ IsBalancedBip G S k` | `exists_balanced_separator` |","+| `connectedComponent_subset_side` (connectivity step) | `IsEmptyPair G A R`, `C` a connected component of `G.induce (A ∪ R)` | `C ⊆ A ∨ C ⊆ R` | induction on `SimpleGraph.Walk` in the induced graph |","+| `isBalancedBip_iff_components` (L1′ c) | `S.card = k` | `IsBalancedBip G S k ↔` the components of `G.induce (univ \\ S)` admit a grouping into sizes `t`, `N − t` | the previous row; the converse by taking unions |","+| `IsRelax G S k` (def) and `isRelax_of_isBalancedBip` (L1′ b) | `IsBalancedBip G S k` | every component of `G.induce (univ \\ S)` has `card ≤ t` | `connectedComponent_subset_side`, `Finset.card_le_card` |","+| `not_isBalancedBip_threeEdges` (L1′ d) | the graph `3K₂` on `Fin 6`, `k = 0`, `S = ∅` | `IsRelax ∧ ¬ IsBalancedBip` | `decide` |","+| `subset_compl_closedNbhd_iff` (Galois step) | — | `R ⊆ univ \\ closedNbhd G A ↔ Disjoint A R ∧ ∀ u ∈ A, ∀ v ∈ R, ¬ G.Adj u v` | `mem_closedNbhd_iff`, `G.adj_symm` |","+| `closedNbhd_mono` | `A ⊆ A'` | `closedNbhd G A ⊆ closedNbhd G A'` | `Finset.biUnion_subset_biUnion_of_subset_left` |","+| `IsClosedPair G A R` (def) | — | `A = univ \\ closedNbhd G R ∧ R = univ \\ closedNbhd G A` | — |","+| `closure_subset` (L2 i) | `IsEmptyPair G A R` | `A ⊆ univ \\ closedNbhd G (univ \\ closedNbhd G A)` and `R ⊆ univ \\ closedNbhd G A` | `subset_compl_closedNbhd_iff` twice |","+| `closure_isClosedPair` (L2 ii–iii) | `IsEmptyPair G A R` | `IsClosedPair G (univ \\ closedNbhd G (univ \\ closedNbhd G A)) (univ \\ closedNbhd G A)` | `closure_subset`, `closedNbhd_mono`, `subset_compl_closedNbhd_iff` |","+| `sdiff_isClosedPair_eq_inter_boundary` (L2 iv) | `IsClosedPair G A R` | `univ \\ (A ∪ R) = (closedNbhd G A \\ A) ∩ (closedNbhd G R \\ R)` | `mem_closedNbhd_iff` |","+| `closure_card` (L2 v) | `IsEmptyPair G A R`, `t ≤ A.card`, `N − t ≤ R.card`, `(univ \\ (A ∪ R)).card ≤ k` | the same three bounds for the closed pair | `closure_subset`, `Finset.card_le_card`, `Finset.card_sdiff` |","+| `exists_closedPair_of_pathwidth_le` (L1 + L2) | `pathwidth G ≤ k`, `k < n` | `∃ A R, IsClosedPair G A R ∧ IsEmptyPair G A R ∧ t ≤ A.card ∧ N − t ≤ R.card ∧ (univ \\ (A ∪ R)).card ≤ k` | the two rows above and `exists_balanced_separator` |","+| `mem_boundary_coverGraph_iff` (L3 c) | `S : Fin m → Finset C` | `c ∈ closedNbhd (coverGraph S) A \\ A ↔ c ∉ A ∧ ∃ j, c ∈ S j ∧ ∃ a ∈ A, a ≠ c ∧ a ∈ S j` | `coverGraph`'s adjacency, `mem_closedNbhd_iff` |","+| `exists_balanced_separator_cover` (L3 b) | an instance with `mospValue ≤ k + 1`, `k < n` | `exists_balanced_separator` for `mospGraph`, with the empty pair stated as \"no pattern meets both\" | `MOSPGraph.lean`'s equality, `isEmptyPair_coverGraph_iff` |","+","+Obstacle noted for item 10, not for this item: `isBalancedBip_iff_components`","+needs connected components of an induced subgraph as `Finset`s with their","+cardinalities, which the development has not used so far (Mathlib's","+`SimpleGraph.ConnectedComponent` is a quotient type; `Fintype` instances","+exist, the union-of-supports bookkeeping does not). The pair form","+`IsBalancedBip` is what the probabilistic statement of item 08 needs, and","+it is cheap; the component form is for the LDP comparison and can be","+left as a named `Prop` with this obstacle if it does not fit.","+","+### 2.7 Regenerate, size range, kill verdict, what this does not claim","+","+* **Regenerate.** `nice -n 19 python3 reports/scratch/separator_moment/02_lemmas/check.py --max-n 9 --workers 8`","+  (run with 4 workers on 2026-11-01 because the one-minute load was","+  above 24; the log says the wall time); `python3 -m pytest tests/test_separator_moment.py`.","+* **Size range.** Every graph on 1–9 vertices; 20,000 random `G(n, p)`","+  graphs at `n = 10, 11, 12` with `p ∈ {0.15, 0.3, 0.45, 0.6}` (seed = job","+  index); 2,000 random covers with 4–10 customers, `m ∈ {n, 2n}`, 2–4","+  customers per pattern (`generate_structured_instance(n, m, cpp, seed = index)`).","+  The lemmas are for every graph; the counts in the table are for these","+  sizes only.","+* **Kill verdict: not met.** Zero counterexamples to L1, L1′, L2 or L3 on","+  310,266 graphs. The item's premise — that a narrow layout forces a","+  balanced separator, a balanced empty bipartition and a closed pair at the","+  forced sizes — stands; the three structures exist on every graph checked","+  at `k = pw`, and at `k = pw − 1` they exist on about 45% of graphs at","+  7–9 vertices, so at these sizes the *necessary* condition is far from","+  sufficient — the slack the loop is about is already visible on nine","+  vertices, and it is the condition's, not only the union bound's.","+* **Does not claim.** Anything probabilistic: no rate, no constant, no","+  statement about `P_N(E_bip)`. That the closed pair is canonical (the","+  `A`-first and `R`-first fixpoints differ on about a tenth of the pairs","+  checked). That `E_relax` is a cheap relaxation at scale (it differs from","+  `E_bip` on under 2% of graphs at 9 vertices; the rate question is item","+  05's). That the share of graphs with a structure at `k = pw − 1` says","+  anything beyond nine vertices. The Lean rows of §2.6 are statements,","+  not proofs; item 10 proves them or names the obstacle."]}]}],"moreFiles":0,"changedFiles":["/home/al/dev/MOSP/reports/separator_moment.md"]}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_011JhMCDxrLMDnm1ij21eGpE","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
